<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cincinnatus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Lucius Quinctius Cincinnatus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucius Quinctius Cincinnatus was a Roman statesman of the 5th century BCE. According to tradition he was called from ploughing his farm in 458 BCE to serve as dictator, rescued a Roman army trapped by the Aequi on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-algidus">Mount Algidus</a>, and gave up his power after sixteen days to return to the plough. He became the Roman model of civic virtue.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lucius Quinctius Cincinnatus belonged to the rich patrician clan Quinctia. His cognomen means 'the curly haired'. In the late 460s BCE Rome was fending off raids by the Aequi, while the tribune Gaius Terentilius Harsa pressed from 462 BCE for a written code of laws to check the near-regal power of the patrician consuls. According to tradition, Cincinnatus's son Caeso led patrician gangs against the plebeians, was charged with a capital offence in 461 BCE and fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>. Modern historians reject the story that his father was fined into poverty as a later invention.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 460 BCE Appius Herdonius seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a> and the consul Publius Valerius Poplicola was killed in its recovery. Cincinnatus, probably illegally, became suffect consul for the rest of the year and violently opposed the plebeian proposal, which made no progress. In 458 BCE the Aequi broke their treaty and besieged the army of the consul Lucius Minucius Esquilinus Augurinus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-algidus">Mount Algidus</a>. The Senate fell into panic and named Cincinnatus dictator. Senators sent to him are said to have found him ploughing his farm.</p><p>He named Lucius Tarquitius his master of the horse and ordered every man of military age to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campus-martius">Campus Martius</a> with twelve times the usual number of stakes. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-algidus">Mount Algidus</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> used the stakes to besiege the besiegers. Cincinnatus accepted the Aequi's pleas for mercy, requiring that three principal offenders be executed and their leaders delivered in chains, and made the army pass under a yoke of three spears. He then disbanded his army and returned to his farm. Modern historians question particulars, and some consider the military account fictional, noting parallels with later episodes of 380 BCE and 217 BCE.</p><p>He may have been dictator a second time in 439 BCE, against the alleged plot of Spurius Maelius, who was killed by Cincinnatus's master of the horse, Gaius Servilius Ahala. That story may be linked to the earlier dictator only by the clan name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Cincinnatus became a Roman model of civic virtue, a leader who held supreme power no longer than necessary. His story was later invoked for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>, who gave up command of the Continental Army and retired to his farm at Mount Vernon. The Society of the Cincinnati was founded by Henry Knox in 1783, and Cincinnati, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, was named in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucius Quinctius Cincinnatus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Custom required him to resign as soon as his task was done: according to Livy, <b>Lucius Quinctius Cincinnatus</b>, called from his plough in 458 BCE to rescue a Roman army, defeated the Aequi and laid down his office after sixteen days.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…magistrate, named by a consul for a single task and for at most six months; according to Livy, <b>Cincinnatus</b> laid down the office in 458 BCE after sixteen days, and Machiavelli defended it in his Discourses on Livy because it was conferred for a limited time and a…</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li><li><blockquote>Their most famous episode is <b>Cincinnatus's</b> rescue of a trapped Roman army at Mount Algidus in 458 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/aequi">Aequi</a></li></ul></section>
